IMPORT RULES (CRITICAL)
1) Treat “Assignment #” as the unique order_number (string). On import:
   - If Assignment # exists already, update the existing record (upsert) and log activity (“import update”).
   - If not, create new record and log activity (“import create”).

2) Map CSV fields to database:
- orders.order_number = Assignment #
- orders.client = Program Name (and ALSO store Program Name separately as a custom field cf:program_name if needed)
- orders.insured_name = Insured Name
- orders.phone = if present in Contact field OR parse from Special Instructions (see below)
- orders.address1 = Address
- orders.city = City
- orders.state = State
- orders.zip = Zip Code
- orders.status = normalize:
     “Work In Progress” -> Active
     “Completed” -> Completed
     “On Hold” -> OnHold
     otherwise default Active
- orders.due_date = Due Date
- orders.notes = Special Instructions (full text preserved exactly)

Additionally store these CSV columns as CUSTOM FIELDS (not core columns):
- Date Assigned -> cf:date_assigned (date)
- DBA Name -> cf:dba_name (string)
- Contact -> cf:contact_name (string)
- Business Desc -> cf:business_desc (string)
- Priority -> cf:priority (string)
- Appointment -> cf:appointment (date if parseable, else string)
- Consultant Name -> cf:consultant_name (string)
- Program Name -> cf:program_name (string)

3) Custom fields support:
- Custom fields are arbitrary key/value with types string/number/boolean/date.
- Use the database model with custom_fields_definitions + order_custom_fields.

SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS PARSING (HIGH VALUE)
Special Instructions contains structured facts. Implement a parser that:
A) Extracts contact info if present in patterns like:
   “Contact Information: Name, 2704016136, email@example.com”
   - If phone found, set orders.phone (normalize digits) and also cf:contact_phone
   - If email found, store cf:contact_email
B) Extracts key facts written as “Label - Value” or “Label: Value”, including comma-separated items:
   Examples from my data:
   - “Protection class - 9”
   - “Year built - 1982”
   - “Total Area - 3500”
   - “Sales - 200,000”
   - “Construction type - Joisted Masonry”
   - “Building Improvements - Wiring - 2010 , Roofing - 2025 , Plumbing - 2010 , Heating - 2010”
C) For each extracted item:
   - Create (or reuse) a custom field definition using a normalized key:
       “Protection class” -> protection_class
       “Year built” -> year_built
       “Total Area” -> total_area
       “Construction type” -> construction_type
       “Sales” -> sales
   - Infer type conservatively:
       numbers with commas -> number
       4-digit year -> number (or date year stored as number)
       otherwise string
D) For the “Building Improvements …” clause:
   - Store as separate custom fields:
       improvements_wiring = 2010
       improvements_roofing = 2025
       improvements_plumbing = 2010
       improvements_heating = 2010

IMPORTANT: Never lose the original text — keep orders.notes = full Special Instructions.

APP REQUIREMENTS (Airtable-like)
- Table-first interface with:
  - Inline edit for core fields + custom fields
  - Column visibility picker (include custom fields as selectable columns)
  - Sort + filter builder that supports:
      - core fields
      - custom fields (by key)
      - “Needs geocode” filter (lat/lon missing)
  - Saved views: store filters + visible columns + sort
- Map view shows ONLY the rows returned by the currently selected view.
- One-click “Complete” in table row or map popup:
  - sets status=Completed, completed_at=now
  - removes it from map and Active-type views instantly
- Activity log:
  - record create/update/status_change/import actions with before/after JSON
- Geocoding:
  - Use Nominatim with caching in SQLite table geocode_cache keyed by normalized full address.
  - “Geocode now” per row and “Bulk geocode current view” button.
- Routing:
  - Start location set by address in Settings (geocode & store)
  - Optimize route for the currently visible orders with lat/lon using OSRM (configurable base URL)
  - Draw route polyline and show ordered stop list with totals
  - “Open in Google Maps” link with waypoints best-effort

